  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday)\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThere is high confidence that above-normal temperatures will \npersist through at least Friday, reinforced by bias-corrected \nprobabilistic guidance indicating that even the 10th percentile \nremains above normal during this period. Global deterministic \nmodels and their ensemble members are in strong agreement that the\nsubtropical ridge will remain the primary influence on our \nweather.\n\nEnsemble spread increases among the global models and their \nensemble members by the weekend into next week (September 19-21), \nwhich indicates the potential for a pattern change. There is a \nchance the subtropical ridge will break down as a mid-level trough\ndigs across the Plains, driving a cold front southward into the \nSouthern Plains.\n\nSunday currently appears to be the most probable timeframe for \nthe cold front passage. That said, deterministic and ensemble \nguidance over the past couple days has shown a distinct trend \ntoward a slower frontal timing. Probabilistic guidance highlights \nthis forecast uncertainty, indicating a 20 to 75% probability of \nmaximum temperatures remaining below 90 deg F on Sunday (20% \nsoutheast to 75% north). The cooler end of guidance reflects a low\nto moderate probability (5% southeast to 40% north) of high \ntemperatures staying below 80 deg F. Nevertheless, a reasonable \nchance persists for high temperatures to top at least 90 deg F \n(25% north to 80% southeast) should the frontal timing continue to\nslow down. The first day of fall is Tuesday, September 22, so \nperhaps we'll see a timely front sometime by then.\n\nMahale",
